{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Test.Harness` - one child per case, and one child per shard - Design-Test.md §5.3 and §6.
   ==========================================================================================

   **Isolation and parallelism are one mechanism.** `--isolate` is `--shard i/n` with `n` the number
   of cases, so there is one code path here and it is "re-execute this binary with a narrower
   selection and read what it says". That is also why the harness needs nothing but `spawn`, `wait`,
   the command line and a place to put the report - which is exactly F4.

   Three things this buys that an in-process run cannot have:

    - **expected aborts.** `@test(aborts)` runs in a child whatever the run's default is, and the
      verdict is inverted: the case passes when the child stops with the abort status and fails when
      it returns normally. That is what makes `assert`, every bounds check and the division rule
      testable at all - a failed check in an in-process driver kills the driver, which is why
      `YanaLibTest` forks today;
    - **crash attribution.** A child that died leaves a `begin` with no `end`, and the parent names
      the case. Free, because the stream was going to be written anyway;
    - **timeouts.** A case that runs past the limit is killed, reported as a timeout, and the run
      continues. Only a parent can do this, and an in-process run should not pretend to.

   > **One deviation from §5.3, and the reason is the timeout.** The design says the child's report
   > comes back over a *pipe*. It comes back through a **file** here. A pipe would have to be read
   > while the child runs - 64 KiB of unread report is a child blocked on its own write - and
   > reading it while also polling for a timeout needs a non-blocking descriptor, which is an
   > `fcntl` this library does not have. A file has no such limit, is read once the child is gone,
   > and loses nothing that matters: the property the design asks of the stream is that a *truncated*
   > report still names the case, and a file truncated by a crash is truncated in exactly the same
   > place a pipe would have been.
-}

import Process
import File
import Environment
import Native
import Terminal

{-
   One case, in a child of its own.

   `Ok` is a pass and carries whatever the parent wants to add to the line - "aborted, as expected".
   `Err` is a failure and carries the sentence that says why, which is the one thing the parent knows
   and the child could not say: it is dead.
-}
{-
   Whether this *process* can put a case in a child at all.

   Not a property of the target, which is what it looks like: a harness is this binary re-executing
   itself, so it needs a path to re-execute - and a program the kernel did not start has none. That
   is not a hypothetical either. `YanaLibTest` JITs a fixture and calls into it, so no entry stub
   ran, no command line was written, and `argument(0)` is `Nothing`; the same fixture built as an
   executable and started by the kernel has one. So the question is asked of the process rather than
   answered by the target, and a runner with no harness *skips* an `@test(aborts)` case and refuses
   `--isolate` - two different answers, and both better than pretending.
-}
pub fn harnessAvailable() -> Bool = ownProgram() is Just(_)

pub fn runChild(name: String, aborts: Bool, options: Options) -> Result(String, String):
    match ownProgram():
        Nothing -> return Err("this build cannot re-execute itself - the command line is empty, so there is no path to spawn")
        Just(path) -> return runOneChild(path, name, aborts, options)

fn runOneChild(path: String, name: String, aborts: Bool, options: Options) -> Result(String, String):
    let reportPath = temporaryPath("case.report")
    let errorPath = temporaryPath("case.output")

    -- `--allow-empty`, because a child whose one name matched nothing is a fact the *parent* reports
    -- against the case it asked for. A child that failed the empty-selection guard would report the
    -- guard rather than the missing case.
    let &arguments = ["--only", copy(name), "--allow-empty"]
    passSnapshotFlags(arguments, options)

    match spawnReporting(path, arguments, reportPath, errorPath, True):
        Err(reason) -> return Err("could not start a child: {describeError(reason)}")

        Ok(child):
            let outcome = awaitChild(child, options.timeoutMs)
            let report = collectReport(reportPath)
            return verdictOf(name, aborts, outcome, report, collectComplaint(errorPath))

{-
   The shards, all of them at once - §6.

   Each child is this binary with `--shard i/n` and the run's own filter, writing its report where
   the parent can read it and its human lines straight to the terminal. The parent's answer is the
   worst of theirs, which is what a suite's status is.

   Round-robin rather than contiguous, and the runner is what applies it - see `runMain`. A shard
   that dies loses one shard: the parent sees the missing `end`, names the case, and the other
   children finish. Today a crashed shard is a red run that says nothing.
-}
pub fn runShards(cases: [Case], options: Options) -> Int:
    match ownProgram():
        Nothing:
            print("this build cannot re-execute itself, so --jobs has nothing to spawn\n")
            return 2

        Just(path):
            let terminal = consoleTerminal(options)
            let &children = [] :: [Process]
            let &paths = [] :: [String]
            let &errorPaths = [] :: [String]

            for i in 0 .. options.jobs:
                let reportPath = temporaryPath("shard{i}.report")
                let errorPath = temporaryPath("shard{i}.output")
                -- Not `-q`: a shard reports its own failures as they happen, and the parent adds only
                -- the aggregate and whatever the shard was still inside when it died.
                let &arguments = ["--shard", "{i}/{options.jobs}", "-q", "--allow-empty"]

                if length(options.filter) > (0 :: Size):
                    push(arguments, "--filter")
                    push(arguments, copy(options.filter))

                if options.verbose then push(arguments, "-v")
                if options.isolate then push(arguments, "--isolate")
                passSnapshotFlags(arguments, options)

                match spawnReporting(path, arguments, reportPath, errorPath, False):
                    Err(reason):
                        print("could not start shard {i}: {describeError(reason)}\n")
                        return 2

                    Ok(child):
                        push(children, child)
                        push(paths, reportPath)
                        push(errorPaths, errorPath)

            let &ran = 0 :: Int
            let &failed = 0 :: Int

            for j in 0 .. length(children):
                let outcome = awaitChild(children[j], 0)
                let report = collectReport(paths[j])

                -- A shard's own human lines are `print`, which is standard output and is inherited;
                -- this is the other channel, which carries what the process said on its way down.
                let said = collectComplaint(errorPaths[j])

                ran = ran + report.ran
                failed = failed + report.failedCases

                -- The shard said nothing for a human - its report went to a file of this parent's
                -- choosing, which is what makes it a child - so the names come from the stream.
                for failed in items(report.failedNames):
                    let mark = terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), "FAIL")
                    print("  {mark} {failed}\n")

                -- The case that was open when the shard stopped, which is the only place this can be
                -- said. §1.2 is precisely this line not existing.
                let mark = terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), "FAIL")

                if length(report.unfinished) > (0 :: Size):
                    failed = failed + 1
                    let why = withComplaint("the shard running it {describeStop(outcome)}", said)
                    print("  {mark} {report.unfinished}: {why}\n")
                else if outcome.timedOut:
                    failed = failed + 1
                    print("  {mark} shard {j}: it did not finish inside the timeout\n")

            if length(cases) == (0 :: Size) then return 1

            let g = terminal.glyphs()
            let across = terminal.dimmed("across {options.jobs} shards")

            if failed == 0:
                let good = terminal.paint(boldColored(Green), g.pass)
                let count = terminal.inColor(Green, "{ran} passed")
                print("  {good}  {count} {across}\n")
                return 0

            let bad = terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), g.fail)
            let count = terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), "{failed} of {ran} failed")
            print("  {bad}  {count} {across}\n")
            return 1

{-
   The snapshot switches a child inherits - Design-Test.md §7.

   **All of them but `--prune`.** A child reached one case, or one shard, so the snapshots it did not
   compare against are the ones somebody else's child did - and a child that pruned would delete the
   whole corpus's goldens on the strength of its own selection. The parent does not prune either
   while its cases are in children: `runSelected` only asks for the stale scan when the run reached
   everything in this process, which is the same rule stated from the other end.

   `--snapshot-dir` has to be forwarded even when it was never written, because the parent may have
   changed its working directory relative to the child's - it has not, today, and a flag that is
   correct only while that stays true is not worth the saving.
-}
fn passSnapshotFlags(&arguments: Array(String), options: Options) -> {}:
    if options.update then push(arguments, "--update")
    if options.noUpdate then push(arguments, "--no-update")

    if length(options.snapshotDir) > (0 :: Size):
        push(arguments, "--snapshot-dir")
        push(arguments, copy(options.snapshotDir))

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The four things a parent does
   ==========================================================================================
-}

-- What a finished child came to, in the terms a verdict is written in. Not `Exit`, because a child
-- this parent killed is neither of that type's two cases in any way a report should say out loud.
pub data ChildStop {status: Int, signal: Int, timedOut: Bool, broken: Bool}

{-
   Spawning one, with descriptor 3 pointed at a file of its own and its standard error at another.

   The report file is created here rather than in the child, so that a child that never started - a
   failed exec, a missing binary - is still distinguishable from one that started and said nothing:
   the file exists and is empty in the first case and does not exist in the second.

   **What the child says is caught rather than inherited, and that is what a terminal is for.** A
   `@test(aborts)` case dies by design, and what a dying child writes - *"assertion failed:
   arithmetic"*, from `Core`'s own `assert` - went straight onto the parent's screen: into the middle
   of the line the console was rewriting, unattributed, once per run, for a case that was *passing*.
   It is the same collision `muteReport` settles for the report stream, and the same answer - one
   reader for one channel.

   **`silent` is which of the two output channels that covers**, and it is the caller's answer to the
   question `runSelected` already asks. A child narrowed to one case prints nothing for a human by
   construction, so both its streams are caught - `assert` writes through `print`, which is standard
   output, so catching the error stream alone caught nothing at all. A *shard* prints its own
   failures as they happen and its standard output is that report, so only its error stream is.

   A file of its own rather than the report's, because the two are written by different things and a
   report is line-oriented: a message with no trailing newline would run into the next `end` line and
   `readReport` would lose both. What the child said is surfaced by `verdictOf` when the verdict is a
   failure and dropped when it is not, so the message is now attached to the case it came from
   instead of being on the screen next to whichever case was running.
-}
fn spawnReporting(path: String, arguments: [String], reportPath: String, errorPath: String,
                  silent: Bool) -> Result(FileError, Process):
    let &into = openFile(reportPath, writeAccess(), CreateAlways)?
    let &complaints = openFile(errorPath, writeAccess(), CreateAlways)?

    let &output = Nothing :: Maybe(File)
    if silent then output = Just(borrowFile(complaints.handle))

    let target = Redirect {input: Nothing, output: output,
                           errors: Just(borrowFile(complaints.handle)),
                           report: Just(borrowFile(into.handle))}

    let started = spawnWith(path, arguments, target)

    {-
       **The close is load-bearing and is not tidiness.**

       `borrowFile(into.handle)` makes a `File` out of a *number*, so nothing connects the descriptor
       in the `Redirect` back to the owner it came from - and a value whose last use has gone past is
       dropped there rather than at the end of the scope, which for a `File` is the descriptor being
       closed. Written without this line, `into`'s last use is the `.handle` above, the file is shut
       before the fork, and the child's `dup2` fails silently: every report came back empty and every
       isolated case read as "the child ran no case called ...".

       So this both keeps the owner alive across the spawn - which is what makes the child's
       descriptor 3 real - and hands the parent's own copy back, which it has no further use for.
    -}
    close(into)
    close(complaints)
    return started

{-
   Waiting, with or without a limit.

   A timeout of zero is no timeout and blocks, which is the ordinary case and the one that costs
   nothing. With a limit, the wait is a poll: `finished` is `wait4` with `WNOHANG`, `monotonicNanos`
   says how long it has been, and the interval between two polls is a millisecond of sleep - which is
   what keeps a case that is doing real work from being charged a whole core to watch it.

   The kill is `SIGKILL` after `SIGTERM` rather than instead of it: a test binary that installed no
   handler dies on the first, and one that did gets the chance to say something. Both are followed by
   a blocking wait, because a killed child still has to be reaped.
-}
fn awaitChild(child: Process, timeoutMs: Int) -> ChildStop:
    if timeoutMs <= 0 then return stopOf(waitFor(child), False)

    -- The parentheses are not decoration: `widen(x) :: I64 * 1000000` reads the multiplication
    -- into the type annotation, and the limit came out as zero - so every case timed out at once.
    let limit = (widen(timeoutMs) :: I64) * 1000000
    let started = monotonicNanos()
    let &interval = ClockTime {seconds: 0, nanos: 1000000}

    while monotonicNanos() - started < limit:
        match finished(child):
            Err(_) -> return ChildStop {status: 0, signal: 0, timedOut: False, broken: True}
            Ok(Just(->exit)) -> return stopOf(Ok(exit), False)
            Ok(Nothing) -> sleepFor(interval)

    signal(child, terminateSignal)
    signal(child, killSignal)
    return stopOf(waitFor(child), True)

fn stopOf(answer: Result(FileError, Exit), timedOut: Bool) -> ChildStop = match answer:
    Err(_) -> ChildStop {status: 0, signal: 0, timedOut: timedOut, broken: True}
    Ok(Exited(status)) -> ChildStop {status: status, signal: 0, timedOut: timedOut, broken: False}
    Ok(Signalled(number)) -> ChildStop {status: 0, signal: number, timedOut: timedOut, broken: False}

-- The report, read once the child is gone, and the file removed behind it. A report that could not
-- be read is an empty one rather than an error: the child's *status* is still an answer, and a
-- parent that refused to report a crash because it also could not read the crash's report would be
-- losing the one thing it does know.
fn collectReport(path: String) -> ChildReport:
    let report = match readWholeFile(path):
        Ok(text) -> readReport(text)
        Err(_) -> emptyReport()

    removeFile(path)
    return report

{-
   What the child wrote to its standard error, as the one line a verdict can carry.

   The first line and not all of it: this ends up inside a report line, and a child that printed a
   hundred lines of its own before it died should not turn one failure into a hundred. The rest is
   not lost in any way that matters - it went where the child was told to put it, and the caller
   knows the path.
-}
fn collectComplaint(path: String) -> String:
    let &said = match readWholeFile(path):
        Ok(text) -> trim(text)
        Err(_) -> ""

    removeFile(path)

    match findAscii(said, 10):
        Nothing -> return said
        Just(at) -> return trimEnd(String.slice(said, 0, at))

{-
   What all of that came to, for one case.

   **The `aborts` verdict is inverted**, which is the whole of §5.3's third row: the case passes when
   the child stopped the way `checkFailed` stops - status 134 natively, which is what a process
   killed by `SIGABRT` reports - and fails when it returned normally. A child killed by the signal
   itself counts too, since whether an abort arrives as a status or as a signal depends on what is
   between the process and its parent rather than on the program.

   An ordinary case's verdict is the child's own: the failures it recorded, plus the case it was
   still inside if it never came back.
-}
fn verdictOf(name: String, aborts: Bool, stop: ChildStop, report: ChildReport, said: String)
    -> Result(String, String):
    if stop.broken then return Err(withComplaint("the child could not be waited for", said))
    if stop.timedOut then return Err(withComplaint("it did not finish inside the timeout", said))

    if aborts:
        -- The expected abort, whose own dying words are not a failure to report - they are what a
        -- passing case of this kind looks like from outside. See `spawnReporting`.
        if stop.signal == abortSignal || stop.status == abortStatus then return Ok(" (stopped, as expected)")
        if stop.signal != 0 then return Err(withComplaint("it was expected to abort and was killed by signal {stop.signal} instead", said))
        return Err(withComplaint("it was expected to abort and returned {stop.status} instead", said))

    if length(report.unfinished) > (0 :: Size):
        return Err(withComplaint("it {describeStop(stop)} while running {report.unfinished}", said))

    if report.failedClaims > 0 then return Err("{report.failedClaims} of its claims did not hold")
    if stop.signal != 0 then return Err(withComplaint("it {describeStop(stop)}", said))

    -- A child that ran nothing is not a pass. `--only` names one case, so an empty report means the
    -- name reached nothing - a case that was filtered out by something, or a binary that no longer
    -- has it.
    if report.ran == 0 then return Err(withComplaint("the child ran no case called {name}", said))
    if stop.status != 0 then return Err(withComplaint("it exited with status {stop.status} having reported nothing", said))

    return Ok("")

{-
   A verdict with what the child said on the end of it, and the verdict alone when it said nothing.

   Not on the claim count, which is the one failure the report already explains in full: a child that
   recorded its own failures has said everything on the stream, and its standard error is then
   whatever the *case* chose to print rather than anything about why it failed.
-}
fn withComplaint(->verdict: String, said: String) -> String:
    if length(said) == (0 :: Size) then return verdict
    return "{verdict} - it said: {said}"

fn describeStop(stop: ChildStop) -> String:
    if stop.timedOut then return "was killed after the timeout"
    if stop.signal != 0 then return "was killed by signal {stop.signal}"
    return "exited with status {stop.status}"

-- 134 is what `abortProcess` exits with and 6 is `SIGABRT`, which is the same event seen from the
-- two sides of a `wait`. Named here rather than written into the comparison, because "134" in a
-- verdict is the one number in this file that means the library worked exactly as designed.
let abortStatus = 134 :: Int
let abortSignal = 6 :: Int

{-
   Somewhere to put a child's report.

   `TMPDIR` when the environment names one and `/tmp` otherwise, which is the convention every tool
   on this platform already follows. The process id is in the name so that two harnesses running at
   once - which is what `run-tests.sh` does - do not read each other's reports.
-}
fn temporaryPath(tag: String) -> String:
    let directory = match variable("TMPDIR"):
        Just(->named) -> named
        Nothing -> "/tmp"

    -- `joinPath` rather than a separator written here: it is what every other path in this library
    -- is built with, and it is the one that knows a directory that already ends in one is not two.
    return joinPath(directory, "yana-test-{processId()}-{tag}")
